  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}(-\\frac{9}{4(x-5)^{6}})\\,=\\,-\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\cdot \\,{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $5$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14247879/assets/574b270248f2acc2a2eb3403f5626e420f5f16abc6e9e01dcb147df83e5806b5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFinally, the negative factor of $-\\frac{9}{4}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}=-\\infty .\n$$",
